/security-review — loop U5, round 2 — range 4ea824e..ac15e99 — 2026-09-19

NO FINDINGS on 4ea824e..ac15e99. Nothing in ac15e99 (only src/modules/git.rs changed) meets the >80%-exploitable bar.

New file reads / disclosure via superproject() or the dot-name rule: the resolver still reads exactly the same four files as round 1 — `<root>/.git` (git.rs:159/235), `<git_dir>/HEAD` (168), `<git_dir>/commondir` (240), `<common>/config` (283). superproject() (263-270) is a pure component scan of the already-resolved common path; it returns `parts[..=i]`, a prefix of that path, so the config it leads to is under a directory the attacker's pointer already named — no new location becomes readable. The dot-name rule (254-257) only moves `identity` one level up for display; what leaves it is `parent_base` (last two components, 355-362) or the raw root in `--doctor` (app.rs:517-522, through `field()` → sanitize at app.rs:366 and `sanitize_report` at 554). Same low-impact "point at a known local repo, learn its origin org/repo" as round 1; not new.

config_value() vs classify's authority stripping: quotes are removed before classify runs (git.rs:212, 300-316), so a quoted `"https://user:token@github.com/o/r"` hits the same `rsplit_once('@')` at 331 and the authority (credential included) is still discarded. A `#` that survives (quoted, or unquoted with no preceding whitespace, e.g. `https://evil.com#@github.com/x`) can only shift the glyph — host ends up `github.com` — and puts `x` in the name; the name is display-only and sanitized at segments.rs:55. No credential, no path outside the URL, reaches output. Cosmetic misclassification at worst.

Panic paths: config_value on `""` → `value[..0]`; on `"` alone → strip_prefix gives "", split_once fails, loop sets end=1 (char boundary), returns `"`; on `#` → end=0, returns "". `char_indices` guarantees `end` is a boundary. superproject: all indexing via `parts.get` except `parts[..i]`/`parts[..=i]` where `i < parts.len()` from the `find`; empty path → None. origin_url header split uses `unwrap_or`. identity_root: `..`-only commondir goes through `normalise` (405-419), which drops `..` above `/` and never panics; `common.parent()` on `/` is None → falls to `common.clone()`. The only `unwrap()`s in non-test code are the pre-existing thread joins at 98-99 (`unwrap_or`).

Subprocess / canonicalize: none added. `canonicalize` appears only in doc comments (225, 400). The only `run_bounded` calls are the pre-existing ahead_count/dirty_counts (369-395), fixed argv, cwd=root, unchanged.
